<section class="hero">
  <div class="hero-inner">
    <div class="eyebrow">What is SFML</div>
    <h1>A kind of script for codifying how you build software</h1>
    <p>
      SFML isn't really a file format so much as a script — the same way Terraform isn't
      really a file format so much as a way of writing down infrastructure so it can be
      diffed, checked, and run. You use SFML to write down a <em>process</em>, not to store
      data. Codifying the process is the whole point: it's what lets you stop re-deciding
      how a piece of work gets done and start improving on a version of it you can see.
    </p>
    <p>
      Concretely, a factory written in SFML describes a durable, resumable, mostly-autonomous
      pipeline: a graph of steps and routing that takes an intent — a ticket, say — and
      carries it through to a reviewed artifact. The graph is defined separately from
      whatever agent runtime actually executes it, so it can be diffed in a pull request,
      linted before it runs, and moved between tools.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="hero example">
  <div class="hero-inner">
    <div class="eyebrow">A small example</div>
    <h2>What a factory actually looks like</h2>
    <p>
      Here's a minimal one: an agent writes a draft, a human decides whether it's ready, and
      once it is, another agent publishes it. Anything short of approval loops back to the
      draft step — and because SFML requires <code>max_iterations</code> on every step, that
      loop can't run forever.
    </p>
    <div class="example-grid">
      <pre class="example-code"><code class="language-yaml">sfml: "v0.1"
description: >
  Turns a rough idea into a published post.
start: draft
budget: 20.00
parameters:
  topic:
    type: string
    description: What to write about.

steps:
  draft:
    type: agent
    harness: claude-agent-sdk
    prompt_path: prompts/draft.md
    prompt_vars:
      topic: parameters.topic
      notes: last(results.review).notes
    max_iterations: 5
    result_schema:
      type: object
      required: [draft_path]
      properties:
        draft_path: { type: string }
    next:
      - to: review

  review:
    type: human
    assignee: "editor"
    instructions: "'Read the draft and decide whether it is ready to publish.'"
    max_iterations: 5
    result_schema:
      type: object
      required: [decision]
      properties:
        decision: { type: string, enum: [approve, revise] }
        notes: { type: string }
    next:
      - when: "last(results.review).decision == 'revise'"
        to: draft
      - to: publish

  publish:
    type: agent
    harness: claude-agent-sdk
    prompt_path: prompts/publish.md
    prompt_vars:
      draft_path: last(results.draft).draft_path
    max_iterations: 3
    result_schema:
      type: object
      required: [ok]
      properties:
        ok: { type: boolean }
    next:
      - to: success

  success:
    type: result
    outcome: complete
    value: last(results.publish)</code></pre>
      <div class="example-diagram">
        <pre class="mermaid">
flowchart TD
  __start__(("start"))
  __start__ --> draft
  draft["draft&lt;br/&gt;agent: claude-agent-sdk"]
  review[/"review&lt;br/&gt;human: editor"/]
  publish["publish&lt;br/&gt;agent: claude-agent-sdk"]
  success(["success&lt;br/&gt;result: complete"])
  draft --> review
  review -->|"decision == 'revise'"| draft
  review --> publish
  publish --> success
  classDef agentStep fill:#dbeafe,stroke:#1d4ed8,color:#1e3a8a;
  classDef humanStep fill:#fef3c7,stroke:#b45309,color:#78350f;
  classDef resultStep fill:#dcfce7,stroke:#15803d,color:#14532d;
  class draft agentStep;
  class review humanStep;
  class publish agentStep;
  class success resultStep;
        </pre>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="example-caption">
      That diagram isn't hand-drawn — it's what
      <a href="https://github.com/craftmilldev/sfml/blob/main/tools/sfml-to-mermaid.mjs"><code>tools/sfml-to-mermaid.mjs</code></a>
      renders straight from a factory file, the same way it keeps this repo's own
      <a href="https://github.com/craftmilldev/sfml/blob/main/.sfml/factory.mmd"><code>.sfml/factory.mmd</code></a>
      in sync with <a href="https://github.com/craftmilldev/sfml/blob/main/.sfml/factory.sfml"><code>.sfml/factory.sfml</code></a>
      — the factory this project uses to build itself.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="hero">
  <div class="hero-inner">
    <div class="eyebrow">How to use it</div>
    <p>
      This repository ships one example implementation, in
      <a href="https://github.com/craftmilldev/sfml/tree/main/example"><code>example/</code></a>
      — a CLI and library that parse, lint, and run a factory. It is merely an example. It
      is not the point of the project, and its behavior isn't normative; the
      <a href="/spec/">spec</a> is.
    </p>
    <p>
      The goal is to give people who build these tools a clear, shared data format so they
      can spend their effort building good tools instead of each reinventing a factory
      format from scratch.
    </p>
    <p>
      To try it, fork the repo and install and run the example tool — see
      <a href="https://github.com/craftmilldev/sfml/tree/main/example">example/README.md</a>
      for instructions. To read the format itself, see the <a href="/spec/">full spec</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you're an LLM, or you're pointing one at this project, start at
      <a href="/llms.txt"><code>/llms.txt</code></a> instead of this page.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

